## Why This Is Asked

Tests integration of fetch, WebSocket, Service Worker, and offline persistence in a realistic UI while focusing on DOM efficiency.

## Key Concepts

- Ordered patch application with per-patch IDs and timestamps
- Service Worker caching for REST data and assets with stale-while-revalidate
- WebSocket reconnect strategy and backoff
- IndexedDB as an offline queue with idempotent replay and conflict checks

## Code Example

```javascript
function applyPatches(root, patches){
  // patches: [{id, type, selector, value, ts}]
  patches.sort((a,b)=> a.id - b.id);
  for(const p of patches){
    // minimal DOM update logic depending on type
    const el = root.querySelector(p.selector);
    if(!el) continue;
    switch(p.type){
      case 'update': el.textContent = p.value; break;
      case 'move': el.style.transform = `translate(${p.value.x}px, ${p.value.y}px)`; break;
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test patch ordering under reconnect bursts?
- How would you handle patch duplication or lost patches in edge cases?

## Why This Is Asked

This angle tests practical control of performance budgets at runtime, tying Lighthouse-like goals to per-route bundling and lazy-loading decisions in production.

## Key Concepts

- Per-route budgets and dynamic import gating
- PerformanceObserver traces for TTI, LCP, long tasks
- Resource hints (preload/prefetch) and idle-time loading
- CI Lighthouse integration and field data correlation

## Code Example

```javascript
// budget gate per route
const ROUTE_BUDGETS = { home: 280000, dashboard: 320000 };
let usedByRoute = {};
function safeLoad(route, path, sizeEstimate) {
  const budget = ROUTE_BUDGETS[route] ?? 300000;
  const used = usedByRoute[route] || 0;
  if (used + sizeEstimate > budget) {
    return import(`./${path}.lite.js`);
  }
  usedByRoute[route] = used + sizeEstimate;
  return import(`./${path}.js`);
}
```

